{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788619850464,"sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_WoHyMM8KILvPK78jDaBz7lxj","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/communication.md","offset":1,"limit":350},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/communication.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: one idea per sentence, verdict first, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n6:   - \"atomic paragraphs\"\n7:   - \"vary the rhythm\"\n8:   - \"plain words\"\n9:   - \"cut filler\"\n10:   - \"empty opener\"\n11:   - \"corporate language\"\n12:   - \"marketing language\"\n13:   - \"match length to the question\"\n14:   - \"wall of text\"\n15:   - \"lead with the recommendation\"\n16:   - \"verdict first\"\n17:   - \"ask then do framing\"\n18:   - \"restate the request\"\n19:   - \"change modality not volume\"\n20:   - \"I don't follow\"\n21:   - \"in simple terms\"\n22:   - \"em dash\"\n23:   - \"remove the dashes\"\n24:   - \"punctuation\"\n25:   - \"too abstract\"\n26:   - \"name the failure a best practice prevents\"\n27:   - \"required versus optional\"\n28: importance_tier: important\n29: contextType: reference\n30: version: 1.0.0.0\n31: ---\n32: \n33: # Rule: Communication\n34: \n35: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n36: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n37: \n38: ## Fires when\n39: \n40: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n41: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n42: - About to answer a complex or ambiguous request.\n43: - The reader has signalled they did not understand.\n44: \n45: This file carries what `AGENTS.md` §8 used to hold in full. Its trigger is deliberately\n46: the broadest in the set: a rule about how replies read has to load whenever a reply is\n47: being written, or it silently stops applying to the short answers that need it most.\n48: \n49: ## The rule\n50: \n51: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: one idea per sentence, the verdict first,\n52: nothing in the reply that does not carry information.**\n53: \n54: Delivery, not rigor. Nothing here licenses a softer claim than the evidence supports.\n55: \n56: ---\n57: \n58: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n59: \n60: Two registers, and [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §6 owns the\n61: distinction: clipped while working, dense at a boundary. What belongs here is picking\n62: correctly, because the common error is not a bad register but the wrong one.\n63: \n64: You are at a **boundary** whenever the reader is about to decide something, act on\n65: something, or take the work over. Everything else is working. A boundary reply that\n66: reads like working notes buries the verdict; working narration written as a boundary\n67: report costs the reader a page to learn you ran `grep`.\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## 2. SENTENCES AND PARAGRAPHS\n72: \n73: **One idea per sentence.** Short, declarative, subject-verb-object where that reads\n74: naturally. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it, nested qualification is where a\n75: reader loses the thread, and it is also where an author hides an unexamined claim.\n76: \n77: **Atomic paragraphs.** Each chunk stands alone. A reader who lands mid-reply should be\n78: able to act on the paragraph in front of them without reconstructing the four above it.\n79: \n80: **Vary the rhythm.** Uniform sentence length reads mechanical, and uniform structure\n81: hides emphasis, if every point is a bullet, no point is more important than any other.\n82: Prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument; the list format implies the\n83: items are independent, and readers believe it.\n84: \n85: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to parse twice.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 3. WORDS\n90: \n91: Plain words by default. Reserve exact names for the things that have them, languages,\n92: frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands, where precision is the point and a synonym\n93: would be wrong.\n94: \n95: Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n96: Three new terms in one paragraph is a paragraph nobody finishes.\n97: \n98: The failure this prevents: the reader stops reading and starts decoding, and stops\n99: noticing whether they agree.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 4. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n104: \n105: **Never use an em dash.** Replace it with a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the\n106: sentence actually wanted. A dash is usually hiding a decision you have not made: an aside\n107: that belongs in commas, a second sentence, or a list that belongs after a colon.\n108: \n109: Two more from the same family:\n110: \n111: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n112: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n113: \n114: **The failure this prevents:** dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n115: reader who has seen a lot of generated text. Either way they cost trust the content earned.\n116: \n117: This rule carries the ban because it fires on every substantive reply. The full standard,\n118: including the vocabulary and structural tells this one does not repeat, is\n119: [`hvr-rules.md`](../.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) in `sk-doc`.\n120: Load it when writing a document rather than a reply.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. LENGTH\n125: \n126: **Match length to the question.** A first answer rarely needs pages. A question that\n127: resolves in three lines gets three lines; opening with a wall of text answers a\n128: question nobody asked and buries the one they did.\n129: \n130: Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there. Effort\n131: spent is not a reason to spend the reader's attention.\n132: \n133: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it.\n134: \n135: ---\n136: \n137: ## 6. CUT FILLER\n138: \n139: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders, each of which reads as\n140: content and is not:\n141: \n142: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n143: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n144: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure.\n145:   If it is worth a warning it is worth naming what goes wrong; see §7.\n146: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n147: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n148: \n149: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n150: sentence that mattered.\n151: \n152: ---\n153: \n154: ## 7. LEAD WITH THE RECOMMENDATION, BUT EARN IT\n155: \n156: State the verdict first, and reach it by analysis. The order of the reply is not the\n157: order of the thinking, and it must not become it: front-loading a conclusion in the\n158: *writing* is a service to the reader, while front-loading one in the *reasoning* is how\n159: you stop noticing the evidence against it.\n160: \n161: If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that, a named uncertainty is a verdict about\n162: the state of the evidence, and it beats a confident sentence you would have to retract.\n163: \n164: The failure this prevents: two of them. A reply the reader must finish before learning\n165: what you think, and a conclusion that got picked early and defended afterwards.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 8. PRESENTING A RECOMMENDATION\n170: \n171: **Recommend one approach.** Name its main trade-off. Mention an alternative only when it\n172: could change the decision, a survey of options the reader will not take is work handed\n173: back rather than done.\n174: \n175: **Separate required from optional.** Mark must-do work distinctly from nice-to-have. A\n176: reader who cannot tell them apart does all of it or none of it.\n177: \n178: **Name the failure a best practice prevents.** Never cite a best practice, guardrail, or\n179: extra layer without stating the specific bug, cost, or user problem it avoids. \"It's\n180: best practice\" is an appeal to authority with the authority left out, and it is how\n181: unnecessary work enters a plan unchallenged, [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md)\n182: is the rule that stops it being built.\n183: \n184: **State assumptions when evidence is missing.** A visible assumption can be corrected by\n185: the reader. A silent one cannot, and it will be discovered as a defect later.\n186: \n187: ---\n188: \n189: ## 9. ASK→DO FRAMING\n190: \n191: For a complex or ambiguous request, preface the answer:\n192: \n193: 1. **ASK:** restate the request in your own words. A paraphrase back, not a question\n194:    back: it proves you understood, and it surfaces a misreading before the work, not after.\n195: 2. **DO:** state your approach in three to seven bullets.\n196: 3. **THEN:** ask only the one or two clarifying questions that would change the\n197:    approach. Consolidate them into a single prompt, per `AGENTS.md` §2; escalate rather\n198:    than guess, per `AGENTS.md` §7.\n199: \n200: A question that would not change what you do is not a clarifying question, it is a delay.\n201: \n202: ---\n203: \n204: ## 10. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n205: \n206: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\", \"too abstract\", \"in simple terms\": all the same signal, and\n207: the wrong response to every one of them is the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: **Change modality, not volume.** Route to `sk-communication`: `/rewrite:response` for\n210: plainer wording, `/rewrite:explain-visually` for a diagram at a chosen depth. That skill\n211: is deliberately held off advisor routing, so this rule is the only thing that reaches\n212: it, §10 below does not waive that.\n213: \n214: The failure this prevents: the second explanation fails the same way as the first,\n215: because it was the same explanation.\n216: \n217: ---\n218: \n219: ## 11. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n220: \n221: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** These shape delivery. Nothing here softens a claim, a\n222:   caveat, or a verification standard owned by\n223:   [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or\n224:   [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n225: - **Not a voice to perform.** Over-constraining voice backfires, it produces answers\n226:   that are hedged, clipped and timid. When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer,\n227:   keep the answer.\n228: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n229:   never about leaving out what they have to know. Cutting a required caveat to look\n230:   concise is a `uncertainty-and-honesty.md` failure wearing this rule as cover.\n231: \n232: ---\n233: \n234: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n235: \n236: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n237: - [ ] The verdict is in the first few lines, and I reached it by analysis rather than committing to it early.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information; no empty opener, restated summary, or unnamed warning survived.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, not what the work cost.\n240: - [ ] Every best practice, guardrail, or extra layer I recommended names the failure it prevents.\n241: - [ ] Required and optional work are visibly distinct.\n242: - [ ] Assumptions I made on missing evidence are stated, not silent.\n243: - [ ] Where the reader said they did not follow, I changed modality rather than adding words.\n244: - [ ] Nothing I cut for concision was something they needed.\n\n(End of file - total 244 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Communication\"\ndescription: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: one idea per sentence, verdict first, nothing that does not carry information.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"one idea per sentence\"\n  - \"atomic paragraphs\"\n  - \"vary the rhythm\"\n  - \"plain words\"\n  - \"cut filler\"\n  - \"empty opener\"\n  - \"corporate language\"\n  - \"marketing language\"\n  - \"match length to the question\"\n  - \"wall of text\"\n  - \"lead with the recommendation\"\n  - \"verdict first\"\n  - \"ask then do framing\"\n  - \"restate the request\"\n  - \"change modality not volume\"\n  - \"I don't follow\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/communication.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Communication\"\ndescription: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: one idea per sentence, verdict first, nothing that does not carry information.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"one idea per sentence\"\n  - \"atomic paragraphs\"\n  - \"vary the rhythm\"\n  - \"plain words\"\n  - \"cut filler\"\n  - \"empty opener\"\n  - \"corporate language\"\n  - \"marketing language\"\n  - \"match length to the question\"\n  - \"wall of text\"\n  - \"lead with the recommendation\"\n  - \"verdict first\"\n  - \"ask then do framing\"\n  - \"restate the request\"\n  - \"change modality not volume\"\n  - \"I don't follow\"\n  - \"in simple terms\"\n  - \"em dash\"\n  - \"remove the dashes\"\n  - \"punctuation\"\n  - \"too abstract\"\n  - \"name the failure a best practice prevents\"\n  - \"required versus optional\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# Rule: Communication\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n- About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n- About to answer a complex or ambiguous request.\n- The reader has signalled they did not understand.\n\nThis file carries what `AGENTS.md` §8 used to hold in full. Its trigger is deliberately\nthe broadest in the set: a rule about how replies read has to load whenever a reply is\nbeing written, or it silently stops applying to the short answers that need it most.\n\n## The rule\n\n**Write so the reader can act after one pass: one idea per sentence, the verdict first,\nnothing in the reply that does not carry information.**\n\nDelivery, not rigor. Nothing here licenses a softer claim than the evidence supports.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n\nTwo registers, and [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §6 owns the\ndistinction: clipped while working, dense at a boundary. What belongs here is picking\ncorrectly, because the common error is not a bad register but the wrong one.\n\nYou are at a **boundary** whenever the reader is about to decide something, act on\nsomething, or take the work over. Everything else is working. A boundary reply that\nreads like working notes buries the verdict; working narration written as a boundary\nreport costs the reader a page to learn you ran `grep`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SENTENCES AND PARAGRAPHS\n\n**One idea per sentence.** Short, declarative, subject-verb-object where that reads\nnaturally. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it, nested qualification is where a\nreader loses the thread, and it is also where an author hides an unexamined claim.\n\n**Atomic paragraphs.** Each chunk stands alone. A reader who lands mid-reply should be\nable to act on the paragraph in front of them without reconstructing the four above it.\n\n**Vary the rhythm.** Uniform sentence length reads mechanical, and uniform structure\nhides emphasis, if every point is a bullet, no point is more important than any other.\nPrefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument; the list format implies the\nitems are independent, and readers believe it.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to parse twice.\n\n---\n\n## 3. WORDS\n\nPlain words by default. Reserve exact names for the things that have them, languages,\nframeworks, APIs, dependencies, commands, where precision is the point and a synonym\nwould be wrong.\n\nIntroduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\nThree new terms in one paragraph is a paragraph nobody finishes.\n\nThe failure this prevents: the reader stops reading and starts decoding, and stops\nnoticing whether they agree.\n\n---\n\n## 4. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n\n**Never use an em dash.** Replace it with a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the\nsentence actually wanted. A dash is usually hiding a decision you have not made: an aside\nthat belongs in commas, a second sentence, or a list that belongs after a colon.\n\nTwo more from the same family:\n\n- **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n- **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n\n**The failure this prevents:** dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\nreader who has seen a lot of generated text. Either way they cost trust the content earned.\n\nThis rule carries the ban because it fires on every substantive reply. The full standard,\nincluding the vocabulary and structural tells this one does not repeat, is\n[`hvr-rules.md`](../.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) in `sk-doc`.\nLoad it when writing a document rather than a reply.\n\n---\n\n## 5. LENGTH\n\n**Match length to the question.** A first answer rarely needs pages. A question that\nresolves in three lines gets three lines; opening with a wall of text answers a\nquestion nobody asked and buries the one they did.\n\nLength is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there. Effort\nspent is not a reason to spend the reader's attention.\n\nThe failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it.\n\n---\n\n## 6. CUT FILLER\n\nEvery sentence carries information. The recurring offenders, each of which reads as\ncontent and is not:\n\n- **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n- **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n- **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure.\n  If it is worth a warning it is worth naming what goes wrong; see §7.\n- **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n- **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n\nThe failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\nsentence that mattered.\n\n---\n\n## 7. LEAD WITH THE RECOMMENDATION, BUT EARN IT\n\nState the verdict first, and reach it by analysis. The order of the reply is not the\norder of the thinking, and it must not become it: front-loading a conclusion in the\n*writing* is a service to the reader, while front-loading one in the *reasoning* is how\nyou stop noticing the evidence against it.\n\nIf you cannot state the verdict yet, say that, a named uncertainty is a verdict about\nthe state of the evidence, and it beats a confident sentence you would have to retract.\n\nThe failure this prevents: two of them. A reply the reader must finish before learning\nwhat you think, and a conclusion that got picked early and defended afterwards.\n\n---\n\n## 8. PRESENTING A RECOMMENDATION\n\n**Recommend one approach.** Name its main trade-off. Mention an alternative only when it\ncould change the decision, a survey of options the reader will not take is work handed\nback rather than done.\n\n**Separate required from optional.** Mark must-do work distinctly from nice-to-have. A\nreader who cannot tell them apart does all of it or none of it.\n\n**Name the failure a best practice prevents.** Never cite a best practice, guardrail, or\nextra layer without stating the specific bug, cost, or user problem it avoids. \"It's\nbest practice\" is an appeal to authority with the authority left out, and it is how\nunnecessary work enters a plan unchallenged, [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md)\nis the rule that stops it being built.\n\n**State assumptions when evidence is missing.** A visible assumption can be corrected by\nthe reader. A silent one cannot, and it will be discovered as a defect later.\n\n---\n\n## 9. ASK→DO FRAMING\n\nFor a complex or ambiguous request, preface the answer:\n\n1. **ASK:** restate the request in your own words. A paraphrase back, not a question\n   back: it proves you understood, and it surfaces a misreading before the work, not after.\n2. **DO:** state your approach in three to seven bullets.\n3. **THEN:** ask only the one or two clarifying questions that would change the\n   approach. Consolidate them into a single prompt, per `AGENTS.md` §2; escalate rather\n   than guess, per `AGENTS.md` §7.\n\nA question that would not change what you do is not a clarifying question, it is a delay.\n\n---\n\n## 10. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n\n\"I don't follow\", \"what?\", \"too abstract\", \"in simple terms\": all the same signal, and\nthe wrong response to every one of them is the same explanation at greater length.\n\n**Change modality, not volume.** Route to `sk-communication`: `/rewrite:response` for\nplainer wording, `/rewrite:explain-visually` for a diagram at a chosen depth. That skill\nis deliberately held off advisor routing, so this rule is the only thing that reaches\nit, §10 below does not waive that.\n\nThe failure this prevents: the second explanation fails the same way as the first,\nbecause it was the same explanation.\n\n---\n\n## 11. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n\n- **Not a constraint on rigor.** These shape delivery. Nothing here softens a claim, a\n  caveat, or a verification standard owned by\n  [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or\n  [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n- **Not a voice to perform.** Over-constraining voice backfires, it produces answers\n  that are hedged, clipped and timid. When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer,\n  keep the answer.\n- **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n  never about leaving out what they have to know. Cutting a required caveat to look\n  concise is a `uncertainty-and-honesty.md` failure wearing this rule as cover.\n\n---\n\n## 12. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n- [ ] The verdict is in the first few lines, and I reached it by analysis rather than committing to it early.\n- [ ] Every sentence carries information; no empty opener, restated summary, or unnamed warning survived.\n- [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, not what the work cost.\n- [ ] Every best practice, guardrail, or extra layer I recommended names the failure it prevents.\n- [ ] Required and optional work are visibly distinct.\n- [ ] Assumptions I made on missing evidence are stated, not silent.\n- [ ] Where the reader said they did not follow, I changed modality rather than adding words.\n- [ ] Nothing I cut for concision was something they needed.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":244,"totalLines":244,"truncated":false}},"title":"repo-rules/communication.md","time":{"start":1788619850384,"end":1788619850445}},"id":"prt_0720d028e001QM83HvC647VBMw","sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","messageID":"msg_0720cd961001O61XZ4w9PEjhWG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788619850464,"sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_LxttUYTlYJVDpIvPIs3wTaB4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md","offset":1,"limit":500},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it; distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22: importance_tier: important\n23: contextType: reference\n24: version: 1.0.0.0\n25: ---\n26: \n27: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n28: \n29: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n30: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n31: \n32: ## Fires when\n33: \n34: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n35: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n36: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n37: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n38: - You are closing out a turn.\n39: \n40: ## The rule\n41: \n42: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n43: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n48: \n49: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n50: |------|---------|--------------------|\n51: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n52: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n53: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n54: \n55: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n56: That is how a confident summary certifies work nobody checked.\n57: \n58: ---\n59: \n60: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n61: \n62: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n63: Not launched. Not assumed. Read.\n64: \n65: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n66: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n67:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n68:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n69: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n70:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n71: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n72:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n77: \n78: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n79:    before the first test body.\n80: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n81:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n82:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n83:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all, and several files\n84:    here use NUL as a composite-key separator. Re-run with `-a` before concluding.\n85: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n86:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n87: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n88:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n93: \n94: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n95: to prove the fix.** Then the same check proves the change. A check that passed before\n96: your change and passes after it proves nothing about your change. If reproducing is\n97: unsafe, say so and name what you used instead.\n98: \n99: ---\n100: \n101: ## 5. BASELINES\n102: \n103: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n104: \n105: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n106:    changing anything.\n107: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n108: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n109: \n110: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n111: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n116: \n117: Three task shapes fail in ways the checks above do not catch:\n118: \n119: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n120:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n121:   you missed, and a clean diff does not reveal an unprocessed input.\n122: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n123:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n124: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n125:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n130: \n131: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n132: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n133: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n134: can explain.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n139: \n140: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n141: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n142: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n143: \n144: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n145: the standard.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n150: \n151: Before any completion claim:\n152: \n153: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n154: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n155: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n156:       output, no unrelated file.\n157: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n158:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n159: \n160: ---\n161: \n162: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n163: \n164: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. Four things, briefly:\n165: \n166: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from §1.\n167: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n168: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n169: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n170:    Four different states, routinely conflated.\n171: \n172: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n173: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n174: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging, the hedging habit devalues the\n175: honest report when it matters.\n176: \n177: ---\n178: \n179: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n180: \n181: Everything above is about proving a claim after the fact. This is about where the claim\n182: came from. Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n183: \n184: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** You have read this pattern a hundred\n185: times, so you know what `resolveConfig` returns. Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n186: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n187: that looks like a different bug.\n188: \n189: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n190: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n191: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n192: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n193: defect.\n194: \n195: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n196: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n197: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope. These are cheap before the edit\n198: and expensive after it, the same asymmetry as §8's proof plan, one step earlier.\n199: \n200: ---\n201: \n202: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n203: \n204: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n205: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n206: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n207: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n208: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n209: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n210: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n\n(End of file - total 210 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\ndescription: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it; distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"observed derived inferred\"\n  - \"how a green run lies\"\n  - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n  - \"negative control\"\n  - \"watch it fail first\"\n  - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n  - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n  - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n  - \"the tool reported success\"\n  - \"claiming done\"\n  - \"final-state proof\"\n  - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n  - \"reason from data not memory\"\n  - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n  - \"honest close-out\"\n  - \"committed pushed dirty\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\ndescription: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it; distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"observed derived inferred\"\n  - \"how a green run lies\"\n  - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n  - \"negative control\"\n  - \"watch it fail first\"\n  - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n  - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n  - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n  - \"the tool reported success\"\n  - \"claiming done\"\n  - \"final-state proof\"\n  - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n  - \"reason from data not memory\"\n  - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n  - \"honest close-out\"\n  - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n  - \"what only the operator can verify\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# Rule: Evidence and proof\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n- About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n- A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n- About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n- You are closing out a turn.\n\n## The rule\n\n**A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\ncarries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. THREE TIERS\n\n| Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n|------|---------|--------------------|\n| **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n| **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n| **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n\nProse that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\nThat is how a confident summary certifies work nobody checked.\n\n---\n\n## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n\n**A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\nNot launched. Not assumed. Read.\n\n- Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n- **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n  start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n  and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n- **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n  the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n- Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n  authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n\n1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n   before the first test body.\n2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n   that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n   is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n   source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all, and several files\n   here use NUL as a composite-key separator. Re-run with `-a` before concluding.\n3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n   metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n   snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n\n---\n\n## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n\n**Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\nto prove the fix.** Then the same check proves the change. A check that passed before\nyour change and passes after it proves nothing about your change. If reproducing is\nunsafe, say so and name what you used instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. BASELINES\n\n\"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n\n1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n   changing anything.\n2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n\nSame for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\ndelta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n\n---\n\n## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n\nThree task shapes fail in ways the checks above do not catch:\n\n- **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n  rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n  you missed, and a clean diff does not reveal an unprocessed input.\n- **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n  Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n- **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n  written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n\n---\n\n## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n\nA sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\nis a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\nunconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\ncan explain.\n\n---\n\n## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n\nFor anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\nacceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\nor inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n\nDeciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\nthe standard.\n\n---\n\n## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n\nBefore any completion claim:\n\n- [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n- [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n- [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n      output, no unrelated file.\n- [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n      blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n\n---\n\n## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n\nEvery substantive turn ends with an honest status. Four things, briefly:\n\n1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from §1.\n2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n   Four different states, routinely conflated.\n\nAnd plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\nwas skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\ndone and verified is stated plainly, without hedging, the hedging habit devalues the\nhonest report when it matters.\n\n---\n\n## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n\nEverything above is about proving a claim after the fact. This is about where the claim\ncame from. Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n\n**Reasoning from what the code probably does.** You have read this pattern a hundred\ntimes, so you know what `resolveConfig` returns. Open it. The cost of being wrong here\nis not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\nthat looks like a different bug.\n\n**Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\na prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\ncode. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n*what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\ndefect.\n\nBefore the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\ndoes something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\nafter you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope. These are cheap before the edit\nand expensive after it, the same asymmetry as §8's proof plan, one step earlier.\n\n---\n\n## 12. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n- [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n- [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n- [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n- [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n- [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n- [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":210,"totalLines":210,"truncated":false}},"title":"repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md","time":{"start":1788619850380,"end":1788619850448}},"id":"prt_0720d028a001cZcljIb9etntSP","sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","messageID":"msg_0720cd961001O61XZ4w9PEjhWG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788619850464,"sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_XaSBdmU9NsBD2AorkawHJrV4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md","offset":1,"limit":500},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Delegation and orchestration\"\n3: description: \"Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model verdict closes a question.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"orchestrate posture\"\n6:   - \"one model is one opinion\"\n7:   - \"second lens\"\n8:   - \"diverge the lens\"\n9:   - \"the brief carries evidence\"\n10:   - \"don't put the conclusion in the brief\"\n11:   - \"accepting a return\"\n12:   - \"the delegate said complete\"\n13:   - \"verify the citation\"\n14:   - \"scope travels with the work\"\n15:   - \"bind write authority\"\n16:   - \"read the executor contract\"\n17:   - \"delegate or not\"\n18:   - \"cheaper to just do it\"\n19:   - \"repair loop for a failed check\"\n20:   - \"two delegates disagree\"\n21:   - \"fan out lineage\"\n22:   - \"sub-agent\"\n23:   - \"CLI executor\"\n24:   - \"deep loop\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.0.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Delegation and orchestration\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before work leaves your hands.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to **decide whether** to hand work to another runtime at all, the cost question\n38:   comes before the briefing question, and this file used to fire only after it.\n39: - About to hand work to another runtime: a CLI executor, a sub-agent, a fan-out lineage, a deep loop.\n40: - About to compose the prompt that a delegate will act on.\n41: - About to accept, quote, or act on what a delegate returned.\n42: - About to answer a judgment question, \"is this the right design\", \"is this rule needed\", \"which approach is better\", from your own reading alone.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **When you hand work to another runtime you become its orchestrator, not its author:\n47: brief it with evidence and a frozen scope, and judge what comes back against the\n48: repository, no single model's verdict, the delegate's or your own, closes a question.**\n49: \n50: Orchestrating is a posture, not a ceremony. It means you own the decomposition, the\n51: brief, and the verdict, and the delegate owns exactly one bounded piece of work.\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 1. THE POSTURE SWITCH\n56: \n57: The moment you delegate, three of your jobs change and one appears.\n58: \n59: | Working alone | Orchestrating |\n60: |---------------|---------------|\n61: | You read the code | You decide what the delegate must read, and say so |\n62: | You hold the scope in your head | You write the scope down, because the delegate cannot see your head |\n63: | Your uncertainty is visible to you | The delegate's confidence tells you nothing about its accuracy |\n64: | Nothing corresponds | You now own a verification step that did not exist before |\n65: \n66: Before any of that, one check: **is delegating cheaper than doing it?** A dispatch costs\n67: a brief, a wait, and a verification pass. Work you could finish in the time it takes to\n68: write the brief is work you should finish, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md).\n69: This is the first question, not a caveat at the end.\n70: \n71: The failure this prevents: dispatching as though the delegate were you with more time.\n72: It is not. It has no memory of this session, and no access to what you already ruled out.\n73: \n74: > **One lens, stated as such.** The claims in this file about how models behave under a\n75: > vague brief are judgment from limited observation, not measurement, the disclosure\n76: > §4 and §6 require of every judgment claim, applied here. What would change them:\n77: > a run where a deliberately underspecified brief returned findings as well-grounded as\n78: > a specified one.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 2. BEFORE YOU DISPATCH\n83: \n84: Five things, in order. None is optional, and the first is a hard rule elsewhere.\n85: \n86: 1. **Read the executor's own contract.** `AGENTS.md` Dispatch Rules require reading\n87:    `cli-external-orchestration/cli-X/SKILL.md` before composing any `cli-X` prompt.\n88:    This file does not repeat what those documents say, and neither should you, a\n89:    dispatch flag copied into prose goes stale the next time the CLI changes.\n90: 2. **Bind the write authority.** Say where the delegate may write before it starts.\n91:    A delegate given the repository is a delegate that will eventually edit something\n92:    nobody reviewed.\n93: 3. **Freeze the scope in the brief itself.** See [`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md).\n94:    Scope you did not write down is scope the delegate will infer, and it will infer wider.\n95: 4. **State the shape of an acceptable answer.** Not the answer, the shape. A file list,\n96:    a table with named columns, a verdict plus citations. \"Investigate X\" returns an essay.\n97: 5. **Pre-resolve every gate the delegate cannot ask you about.** A dispatched worker reads the\n98:    same gates you do and obeys them, including the ones that stop and wait for an operator.\n99:    Nobody is at its prompt, so it stops forever and reports success. Setting the environment\n100:    variable that waives a gate is not enough: it makes the waiver true, not observable, and a\n101:    model cannot read an environment variable. Put the answer in the prompt. Give the decision,\n102:    not permission to skip it.\n103: \n104: ---\n105: \n106: ## 3. A BRIEF CARRIES EVIDENCE, NOT PREFERENCE\n107: \n108: A brief that says *do what you think is best* gets back what the model thinks is best,\n109: which is a sample from a distribution, not a finding about this repository.\n110: \n111: **Put in the brief:** the files and symbols to read, with paths; what has already been\n112: ruled out and why; the constraint that must hold; the format of the return; the\n113: requirement to cite `file:line` for every claim.\n114: \n115: **Keep out of the brief:** your preferred conclusion. If you tell a delegate what you\n116: expect, you have built a machine for confirming it. Ask the question you actually have.\n117: \n118: The failure this prevents: a five-iteration research run that produces a fluent\n119: restatement of the brief's own assumptions, and reads as corroboration.\n120: \n121: ---\n122: \n123: ## 4. ONE MODEL IS ONE OPINION\n124: \n125: A delegate's output is a hypothesis. So is yours.\n126: \n127: For a **factual** question (does this symbol exist, does this test pass, what does this\n128: file say), one delegate is enough, because the repository can settle it and you will\n129: check.\n130: \n131: For a **judgment** question (is this abstraction warranted, is this rule needed, which\n132: of two designs is better), one lens is not a finding. Do one of these, and say which:\n133: \n134: - **Diverge the lens:** ask a second model family, or the same question framed against\n135:   the opposite conclusion.\n136: - **Ground it:** convert the judgment into something the repository can answer. \"Is this\n137:   rule needed\" becomes \"find the commits where its absence cost us something.\"\n138: - **Escalate it:** if it is genuinely a preference, it is the operator's, not the model's.\n139: \n140: Agreement between two runs of the same model is not corroboration. It is the same\n141: opinion twice.\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## 5. WHAT COMES BACK IS UNVERIFIED\n146: \n147: A delegate reporting `COMPLETE` has reported a claim about itself. See\n148: [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §7, a finding is a hypothesis until\n149: something you ran confirms it. Three checks before you quote a return:\n150: \n151: - **Does the citation resolve?** Open one of them. A fabricated `file:line` is the\n152:   cheapest thing to detect and the most expensive thing to propagate.\n153: - **Did it stay in scope?** Diff the paths it touched against the paths you authorized.\n154: - **Did it actually run?** An iteration count from a run's own summary is the run\n155:   describing itself. Read the state it wrote, not the story it told.\n156: - **Do not read the exit status as the verdict.** It has been wrong in both directions:\n157:   a dispatch that stopped at a gate and wrote nothing exits zero, and a dispatch that\n158:   wrote everything then hit a provider capacity limit exits one. Check that the artifacts\n159:   exist, then check their content. The return code tells you the process ended.\n160: \n161: The failure this prevents: a finding entering your close-out with the delegate's\n162: confidence attached and none of your verification.\n163: \n164: **When a check fails, there is a next step and it is not silence.** A return that fails\n165: verification is not discarded and not quoted. Re-dispatch with the brief corrected for\n166: what the failure revealed, or record the failure as the finding, a delegate that could\n167: not answer is evidence about the question. What you may not do is drop it, because the\n168: next reader cannot tell an unasked question from an unanswerable one.\n169: \n170: **Persist your side too.** The delegate's state is written down; yours usually is not.\n171: Keep the brief you sent, the checks you ran on the return, and the verdict you reached,\n172: in whatever artifact owns the work. A verified return with no record of the verification\n173: is indistinguishable, later, from an unverified one.\n174: \n175: **When two delegates disagree, do not average them.** Disagreement means the question was\n176: underspecified or the evidence is genuinely thin. Find which, and say which, a tally is\n177: not a finding.\n178: \n179: ---\n180: \n181: ## 6. YOUR OWN OPINION IS ALSO ONE OPINION\n182: \n183: The rule cuts inward. Skipping delegation because you already know the answer is the\n184: same single-lens failure, minus the paper trail. When you are about to answer a\n185: judgment question from your own reading alone, either ground it in the repository or\n186: say plainly that it is your judgment and what would change it.\n187: \n188: The failure this prevents: shipping a confident design verdict that no evidence\n189: supports, in a session where checking would have cost one dispatch.\n190: \n191: ---\n192: \n193: ## 7. SCOPE TRAVELS WITH THE WORK\n194: \n195: The delegate inherits your frozen scope and cannot widen it. If it comes back having\n196: fixed something adjacent, that is not a bonus, it is an unreviewed change, and it goes\n197: back out or gets raised as an amendment. Widening scope is the operator's call, and\n198: delegating did not transfer that.\n199: \n200: You also stay accountable for what the delegate did. \"The sub-agent wrote it\" is not a\n201: defect report; it is the same sentence as \"not my code\", which\n202: [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) already refuses.\n203: \n204: **Do not commit what you did not read, and remember that staging by name is not enough.**\n205: While a delegate is running, its edits sit in the same working tree as yours. `git add -A`\n206: cannot tell them apart, so it is the obvious trap. The one that actually catches people is\n207: quieter: **`git commit` commits the whole index, not the paths you just added.** A delegate\n208: told to stage its own work as it goes, which is the right instruction, is staging into that\n209: same index. Your next commit takes its files too, under a message about something else,\n210: and the delegate reads the result as you having committed work it was told to leave alone.\n211: \n212: So name the paths at commit time, not only at add time: `git commit -- <paths>` or\n213: `git commit --only <paths>`. Check `git diff --cached --name-only` first when a delegate is\n214: live. Two catches worth knowing before they cost you a commit. A pathspec commit only sees\n215: tracked changes, so a brand new file still needs its `git add` and silently contributes\n216: nothing without one. And a pathspec built from `git diff --cached --name-only` is the whole\n217: index dressed up as a list: it names every file every delegate has staged, and passing it to\n218: `git commit` sweeps them all exactly as if you had passed nothing. Build the pathspec from the\n219: paths you told the delegate to own, and filter the index against those, never the reverse. When a delegate's work does belong in your commit, read it first and say so in the\n220: message.\n221: \n222: ---\n223: \n224: ## 8. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n225: \n226: - **Not a mandate to delegate.** A dispatch that costs more than doing the work is a\n227:   restraint failure, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md). Most tasks are\n228:   cheaper done directly.\n229: - **Not permission to defer the verdict.** The orchestrator decides. A delegate that\n230:   returns three options has returned three options, not a decision.\n231: - **Not a routing document.** Which skill, which agent, which command, which flags, all of that belongs to `AGENTS.md` and the skills it routes to. This file governs\n232:   the posture, not the plumbing.\n233: - **Not about human collaboration.** It addresses machine delegation. The parts that\n234:   generalize do so by accident, not by design.\n235: \n236: ---\n237: \n238: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n239: \n240: - [ ] I read the executor's own contract before composing the prompt.\n241: - [ ] The brief names the files to read, the frozen scope, and the shape of the answer.\n242: - [ ] The brief does not contain the conclusion I expect.\n243: - [ ] Write authority was bound before the delegate started, not corrected after.\n244: - [ ] For every judgment claim I am about to repeat, I diverged the lens, grounded it, or escalated it.\n245: - [ ] I opened every citation I am about to repeat, not a sample, one resolved citation\n246:       proves nothing about the others, and §5 says why that matters.\n247: - [ ] The paths the delegate touched match the paths I authorized.\n248: - [ ] Where I answered from my own reading alone, I said so and said what would change it.\n249: - [ ] Delegating was cheaper than doing it, and I can say why.\n250: - [ ] A failed verification was re-dispatched or recorded, never dropped.\n251: - [ ] My brief, my checks, and my verdict are written down somewhere durable.\n\n(End of file - total 251 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Delegation and orchestration\"\ndescription: \"Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model verdict closes a question.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"orchestrate posture\"\n  - \"one model is one opinion\"\n  - \"second lens\"\n  - \"diverge the lens\"\n  - \"the brief carries evidence\"\n  - \"don't put the conclusion in the brief\"\n  - \"accepting a return\"\n  - \"the delegate said complete\"\n  - \"verify the citation\"\n  - \"scope travels with the work\"\n  - \"bind write authority\"\n  - \"read the executor contract\"\n  - \"delegate or not\"\n  - \"cheaper to just do it\"\n  - \"repair loop for a failed check\"\n  - \"two delegates disagree\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Delegation and orchestration\"\ndescription: \"Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model verdict closes a question.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"orchestrate posture\"\n  - \"one model is one opinion\"\n  - \"second lens\"\n  - \"diverge the lens\"\n  - \"the brief carries evidence\"\n  - \"don't put the conclusion in the brief\"\n  - \"accepting a return\"\n  - \"the delegate said complete\"\n  - \"verify the citation\"\n  - \"scope travels with the work\"\n  - \"bind write authority\"\n  - \"read the executor contract\"\n  - \"delegate or not\"\n  - \"cheaper to just do it\"\n  - \"repair loop for a failed check\"\n  - \"two delegates disagree\"\n  - \"fan out lineage\"\n  - \"sub-agent\"\n  - \"CLI executor\"\n  - \"deep loop\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# Rule: Delegation and orchestration\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before work leaves your hands.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to **decide whether** to hand work to another runtime at all, the cost question\n  comes before the briefing question, and this file used to fire only after it.\n- About to hand work to another runtime: a CLI executor, a sub-agent, a fan-out lineage, a deep loop.\n- About to compose the prompt that a delegate will act on.\n- About to accept, quote, or act on what a delegate returned.\n- About to answer a judgment question, \"is this the right design\", \"is this rule needed\", \"which approach is better\", from your own reading alone.\n\n## The rule\n\n**When you hand work to another runtime you become its orchestrator, not its author:\nbrief it with evidence and a frozen scope, and judge what comes back against the\nrepository, no single model's verdict, the delegate's or your own, closes a question.**\n\nOrchestrating is a posture, not a ceremony. It means you own the decomposition, the\nbrief, and the verdict, and the delegate owns exactly one bounded piece of work.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE POSTURE SWITCH\n\nThe moment you delegate, three of your jobs change and one appears.\n\n| Working alone | Orchestrating |\n|---------------|---------------|\n| You read the code | You decide what the delegate must read, and say so |\n| You hold the scope in your head | You write the scope down, because the delegate cannot see your head |\n| Your uncertainty is visible to you | The delegate's confidence tells you nothing about its accuracy |\n| Nothing corresponds | You now own a verification step that did not exist before |\n\nBefore any of that, one check: **is delegating cheaper than doing it?** A dispatch costs\na brief, a wait, and a verification pass. Work you could finish in the time it takes to\nwrite the brief is work you should finish, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md).\nThis is the first question, not a caveat at the end.\n\nThe failure this prevents: dispatching as though the delegate were you with more time.\nIt is not. It has no memory of this session, and no access to what you already ruled out.\n\n> **One lens, stated as such.** The claims in this file about how models behave under a\n> vague brief are judgment from limited observation, not measurement, the disclosure\n> §4 and §6 require of every judgment claim, applied here. What would change them:\n> a run where a deliberately underspecified brief returned findings as well-grounded as\n> a specified one.\n\n---\n\n## 2. BEFORE YOU DISPATCH\n\nFive things, in order. None is optional, and the first is a hard rule elsewhere.\n\n1. **Read the executor's own contract.** `AGENTS.md` Dispatch Rules require reading\n   `cli-external-orchestration/cli-X/SKILL.md` before composing any `cli-X` prompt.\n   This file does not repeat what those documents say, and neither should you, a\n   dispatch flag copied into prose goes stale the next time the CLI changes.\n2. **Bind the write authority.** Say where the delegate may write before it starts.\n   A delegate given the repository is a delegate that will eventually edit something\n   nobody reviewed.\n3. **Freeze the scope in the brief itself.** See [`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md).\n   Scope you did not write down is scope the delegate will infer, and it will infer wider.\n4. **State the shape of an acceptable answer.** Not the answer, the shape. A file list,\n   a table with named columns, a verdict plus citations. \"Investigate X\" returns an essay.\n5. **Pre-resolve every gate the delegate cannot ask you about.** A dispatched worker reads the\n   same gates you do and obeys them, including the ones that stop and wait for an operator.\n   Nobody is at its prompt, so it stops forever and reports success. Setting the environment\n   variable that waives a gate is not enough: it makes the waiver true, not observable, and a\n   model cannot read an environment variable. Put the answer in the prompt. Give the decision,\n   not permission to skip it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. A BRIEF CARRIES EVIDENCE, NOT PREFERENCE\n\nA brief that says *do what you think is best* gets back what the model thinks is best,\nwhich is a sample from a distribution, not a finding about this repository.\n\n**Put in the brief:** the files and symbols to read, with paths; what has already been\nruled out and why; the constraint that must hold; the format of the return; the\nrequirement to cite `file:line` for every claim.\n\n**Keep out of the brief:** your preferred conclusion. If you tell a delegate what you\nexpect, you have built a machine for confirming it. Ask the question you actually have.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a five-iteration research run that produces a fluent\nrestatement of the brief's own assumptions, and reads as corroboration.\n\n---\n\n## 4. ONE MODEL IS ONE OPINION\n\nA delegate's output is a hypothesis. So is yours.\n\nFor a **factual** question (does this symbol exist, does this test pass, what does this\nfile say), one delegate is enough, because the repository can settle it and you will\ncheck.\n\nFor a **judgment** question (is this abstraction warranted, is this rule needed, which\nof two designs is better), one lens is not a finding. Do one of these, and say which:\n\n- **Diverge the lens:** ask a second model family, or the same question framed against\n  the opposite conclusion.\n- **Ground it:** convert the judgment into something the repository can answer. \"Is this\n  rule needed\" becomes \"find the commits where its absence cost us something.\"\n- **Escalate it:** if it is genuinely a preference, it is the operator's, not the model's.\n\nAgreement between two runs of the same model is not corroboration. It is the same\nopinion twice.\n\n---\n\n## 5. WHAT COMES BACK IS UNVERIFIED\n\nA delegate reporting `COMPLETE` has reported a claim about itself. See\n[`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §7, a finding is a hypothesis until\nsomething you ran confirms it. Three checks before you quote a return:\n\n- **Does the citation resolve?** Open one of them. A fabricated `file:line` is the\n  cheapest thing to detect and the most expensive thing to propagate.\n- **Did it stay in scope?** Diff the paths it touched against the paths you authorized.\n- **Did it actually run?** An iteration count from a run's own summary is the run\n  describing itself. Read the state it wrote, not the story it told.\n- **Do not read the exit status as the verdict.** It has been wrong in both directions:\n  a dispatch that stopped at a gate and wrote nothing exits zero, and a dispatch that\n  wrote everything then hit a provider capacity limit exits one. Check that the artifacts\n  exist, then check their content. The return code tells you the process ended.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a finding entering your close-out with the delegate's\nconfidence attached and none of your verification.\n\n**When a check fails, there is a next step and it is not silence.** A return that fails\nverification is not discarded and not quoted. Re-dispatch with the brief corrected for\nwhat the failure revealed, or record the failure as the finding, a delegate that could\nnot answer is evidence about the question. What you may not do is drop it, because the\nnext reader cannot tell an unasked question from an unanswerable one.\n\n**Persist your side too.** The delegate's state is written down; yours usually is not.\nKeep the brief you sent, the checks you ran on the return, and the verdict you reached,\nin whatever artifact owns the work. A verified return with no record of the verification\nis indistinguishable, later, from an unverified one.\n\n**When two delegates disagree, do not average them.** Disagreement means the question was\nunderspecified or the evidence is genuinely thin. Find which, and say which, a tally is\nnot a finding.\n\n---\n\n## 6. YOUR OWN OPINION IS ALSO ONE OPINION\n\nThe rule cuts inward. Skipping delegation because you already know the answer is the\nsame single-lens failure, minus the paper trail. When you are about to answer a\njudgment question from your own reading alone, either ground it in the repository or\nsay plainly that it is your judgment and what would change it.\n\nThe failure this prevents: shipping a confident design verdict that no evidence\nsupports, in a session where checking would have cost one dispatch.\n\n---\n\n## 7. SCOPE TRAVELS WITH THE WORK\n\nThe delegate inherits your frozen scope and cannot widen it. If it comes back having\nfixed something adjacent, that is not a bonus, it is an unreviewed change, and it goes\nback out or gets raised as an amendment. Widening scope is the operator's call, and\ndelegating did not transfer that.\n\nYou also stay accountable for what the delegate did. \"The sub-agent wrote it\" is not a\ndefect report; it is the same sentence as \"not my code\", which\n[`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) already refuses.\n\n**Do not commit what you did not read, and remember that staging by name is not enough.**\nWhile a delegate is running, its edits sit in the same working tree as yours. `git add -A`\ncannot tell them apart, so it is the obvious trap. The one that actually catches people is\nquieter: **`git commit` commits the whole index, not the paths you just added.** A delegate\ntold to stage its own work as it goes, which is the right instruction, is staging into that\nsame index. Your next commit takes its files too, under a message about something else,\nand the delegate reads the result as you having committed work it was told to leave alone.\n\nSo name the paths at commit time, not only at add time: `git commit -- <paths>` or\n`git commit --only <paths>`. Check `git diff --cached --name-only` first when a delegate is\nlive. Two catches worth knowing before they cost you a commit. A pathspec commit only sees\ntracked changes, so a brand new file still needs its `git add` and silently contributes\nnothing without one. And a pathspec built from `git diff --cached --name-only` is the whole\nindex dressed up as a list: it names every file every delegate has staged, and passing it to\n`git commit` sweeps them all exactly as if you had passed nothing. Build the pathspec from the\npaths you told the delegate to own, and filter the index against those, never the reverse. When a delegate's work does belong in your commit, read it first and say so in the\nmessage.\n\n---\n\n## 8. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n\n- **Not a mandate to delegate.** A dispatch that costs more than doing the work is a\n  restraint failure, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md). Most tasks are\n  cheaper done directly.\n- **Not permission to defer the verdict.** The orchestrator decides. A delegate that\n  returns three options has returned three options, not a decision.\n- **Not a routing document.** Which skill, which agent, which command, which flags, all of that belongs to `AGENTS.md` and the skills it routes to. This file governs\n  the posture, not the plumbing.\n- **Not about human collaboration.** It addresses machine delegation. The parts that\n  generalize do so by accident, not by design.\n\n---\n\n## 9. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] I read the executor's own contract before composing the prompt.\n- [ ] The brief names the files to read, the frozen scope, and the shape of the answer.\n- [ ] The brief does not contain the conclusion I expect.\n- [ ] Write authority was bound before the delegate started, not corrected after.\n- [ ] For every judgment claim I am about to repeat, I diverged the lens, grounded it, or escalated it.\n- [ ] I opened every citation I am about to repeat, not a sample, one resolved citation\n      proves nothing about the others, and §5 says why that matters.\n- [ ] The paths the delegate touched match the paths I authorized.\n- [ ] Where I answered from my own reading alone, I said so and said what would change it.\n- [ ] Delegating was cheaper than doing it, and I can say why.\n- [ ] A failed verification was re-dispatched or recorded, never dropped.\n- [ ] My brief, my checks, and my verdict are written down somewhere durable.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":251,"totalLines":251,"truncated":false}},"title":"repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md","time":{"start":1788619850369,"end":1788619850452}},"id":"prt_0720d027c001H7AlwZRBkKZpmH","sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","messageID":"msg_0720cd961001O61XZ4w9PEjhWG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788619850464,"sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_bWwryUt2xYF2bHoISUOgA5Te","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md","offset":1,"limit":350},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.0.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n38: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n39: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n40: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n45: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n46: \n47: The naming is the whole rule. \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\"\n48: is not a failure. A failure is something that breaks today, for a requirement that\n49: exists today.\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n54: \n55: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n56: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n57: \n58: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n59: |------|-----------------|\n60: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n61: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n62: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n63: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n64: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n65: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n66: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n67: \n68: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n69: > rung ladder.** For code, `AGENTS.md` §3 names\n70: > `sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` §1 as the authoritative\n71: > rungs, and that ladder orders *solution sources*, standard library, then native\n72: > platform, then an installed dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite\n73: > rung numbers from that file, and cite moves by name from this one. Naming a \"rung 2\"\n74: > here would mean something different there, which is exactly the confusion this section\n75: > stopped causing.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out, > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n79: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n80: > beside it.\"\n81: \n82: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n83: past the move you can justify.\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n88: \n89: After reading the existing code, before the first edit:\n90: \n91: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n92:    its climbing sentence.\n93: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n94:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`), and the contract that must not\n95:    break. No real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code\n96:    should not exist either.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n101: \n102: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n103: \n104: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n105: |--------|---------------|---------|\n106: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n107: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n108: \n109: ---\n110: \n111: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n112: \n113: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n114: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n115: real callers need different values today**.\n116: \n117: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n118: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication, and duplication\n119: is cheaper to fix than the wrong seam.\n120: \n121: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n122: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n123: \n124: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n125: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n126: reader it *is* sometimes null, and they will code around a ghost.\n127: \n128: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n129: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n130: \n131: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n132: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n133: \n134: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n135: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n136: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n137: case\" is not an environment.\n138: \n139: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one is the costliest move in §1,\n140: needs its climbing sentence, and takes the `blast-radius.md` pass too, installing\n141: mutates the environment.\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n146: \n147: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n148: \n149: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n150:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n151: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n152:   speculative.\n153: \n154: ---\n155: \n156: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n157: \n158: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n159: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n160: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n161: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n162: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n\n(End of file - total 162 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\ndescription: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"restraint ladder\"\n  - \"reversal cost order\"\n  - \"does this need to exist\"\n  - \"build nothing\"\n  - \"future proof\"\n  - \"might need it later\"\n  - \"flexible\"\n  - \"best practice\"\n  - \"while we're here\"\n  - \"premature abstraction\"\n  - \"DRY it up\"\n  - \"two is not a pattern\"\n  - \"speculative optimization\"\n  - \"add a config option\"\n  - \"add a dependency\"\n  - \"feature flag\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\ndescription: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"restraint ladder\"\n  - \"reversal cost order\"\n  - \"does this need to exist\"\n  - \"build nothing\"\n  - \"future proof\"\n  - \"might need it later\"\n  - \"flexible\"\n  - \"best practice\"\n  - \"while we're here\"\n  - \"premature abstraction\"\n  - \"DRY it up\"\n  - \"two is not a pattern\"\n  - \"speculative optimization\"\n  - \"add a config option\"\n  - \"add a dependency\"\n  - \"feature flag\"\n  - \"new abstraction layer\"\n  - \"pre-write pass\"\n  - \"climbing sentence\"\n  - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# Rule: Prevent overengineering\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n- Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n- Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n- Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n\n## The rule\n\n**Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\nafter naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n\nThe naming is the whole rule. \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\"\nis not a failure. A failure is something that breaks today, for a requirement that\nexists today.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n\nStart at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\nthat says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n\n| Move | Cost when wrong |\n|------|-----------------|\n| **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n| Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n| Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n| Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n| Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n| Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n| Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n\n> **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n> rung ladder.** For code, `AGENTS.md` §3 names\n> `sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` §1 as the authoritative\n> rungs, and that ladder orders *solution sources*, standard library, then native\n> platform, then an installed dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite\n> rung numbers from that file, and cite moves by name from this one. Naming a \"rung 2\"\n> here would mean something different there, which is exactly the confusion this section\n> stopped causing.\n\nBuilding nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\nby code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out, > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n> incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n> beside it.\"\n\nIf you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\npast the move you can justify.\n\n---\n\n## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n\nAfter reading the existing code, before the first edit:\n\n1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n   its climbing sentence.\n2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n   the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`), and the contract that must not\n   break. No real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code\n   should not exist either.\n\n---\n\n## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n\nIts Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n\n| Signal | What it means | Do this |\n|--------|---------------|---------|\n| A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n| A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n\n---\n\n## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n\n**Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\naxis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\nreal callers need different values today**.\n\n**Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\nat two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication, and duplication\nis cheaper to fix than the wrong seam.\n\n**Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\nswallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n\n**Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\nlayer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\nreader it *is* sometimes null, and they will code around a ghost.\n\n**Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\napplies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n\n**Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\nbaseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n\n**Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\nactually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\npath, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\ncase\" is not an environment.\n\n**Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one is the costliest move in §1,\nneeds its climbing sentence, and takes the `blast-radius.md` pass too, installing\nmutates the environment.\n\n---\n\n## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n\nRestraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n\n- **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n  cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n- **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n  speculative.\n\n---\n\n## 6. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n- [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n- [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n- [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n- [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":162,"totalLines":162,"truncated":false}},"title":"repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md","time":{"start":1788619850382,"end":1788619850456}},"id":"prt_0720d028c0019j0RNXe42OR5W8","sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","messageID":"msg_0720cd961001O61XZ4w9PEjhWG"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788619850465,"sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_Ksh9I8Ee38ZIoABSyjLKdwTL","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable; adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.0.0\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Scope discipline\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n36: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n37: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n38: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n39: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n44: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n49: \n50: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n51: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n52: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n53: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n54: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n55: \n56: Narrowing is the most common and least visible, because it is easy to mistake for\n57: restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break:** a change that leaves the tree broken is\n69:   not finished.\n70: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n71: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n72: \n73: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n74: \n75: ---\n76: \n77: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n78: \n79: - Adding a dependency.\n80: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n81: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n82: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n83: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n84: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n85: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n90: \n91: You will find real problems outside scope. Finding them is good; fixing them silently\n92: is not.\n93: \n94: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n95: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n96: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n97: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n98: \n99: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n100: incorrect, in which case it was never adjacent, it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n105: \n106: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n107: file. Read it there. This section deliberately does not restate it: a hard blocker\n108: copied into a tier-3 document reads as though an operator instruction could outrank it.\n109: What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n114: \n115: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n116: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n117: The operator decides.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 7. FINISHING\n122: \n123: `AGENTS.md` §3 Ownership & Completion binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n124: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. Two\n125: things are worth stating in scope terms:\n126: \n127: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n128:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n129: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n130:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n131:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n132: \n133: ---\n134: \n135: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n136: \n137: Scope you have not thought through is scope you will discover mid-edit, which is where\n138: drift starts. Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n139: say them:\n140: \n141: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n142:    pre-write pass, and it is not repeated here, but *which* files is a scope decision,\n143:    because the set you read is the set you will feel entitled to change.\n144: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them up front is what catches\n145:    the case where the plan needs something outside the frozen scope, while it is still\n146:    cheap to raise.\n147: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan;\n148:    what belongs here is that a change with no named check is a change with no defined\n149:    edge, and an undefined edge is where \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n150: \n151: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n152: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n153: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n154: \n155: ---\n156: \n157: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n158: \n159: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n160: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n161: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n162: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n163: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n164: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n\n(End of file - total 164 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\ndescription: \"The requested scope is the deliverable; adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"frozen scope\"\n  - \"scope lock\"\n  - \"scope drift\"\n  - \"out of scope\"\n  - \"in scope\"\n  - \"while I was in there\"\n  - \"noticed in passing\"\n  - \"adjacent defect\"\n  - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n  - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n  - \"rename reformat delete\"\n  - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n  - \"amendment over absorption\"\n  - \"plan before acting\"\n  - \"what needs a yes first\"\n  - \"no early stop\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/044-zvec-grep-integration/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\ndescription: \"The requested scope is the deliverable; adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"frozen scope\"\n  - \"scope lock\"\n  - \"scope drift\"\n  - \"out of scope\"\n  - \"in scope\"\n  - \"while I was in there\"\n  - \"noticed in passing\"\n  - \"adjacent defect\"\n  - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n  - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n  - \"rename reformat delete\"\n  - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n  - \"amendment over absorption\"\n  - \"plan before acting\"\n  - \"what needs a yes first\"\n  - \"no early stop\"\n  - \"natural checkpoint\"\n  - \"good stopping point\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.0.0\n---\n\n# Rule: Scope discipline\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n- The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n- Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n- Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n- Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n\n## The rule\n\n**The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\nAdjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n\n| Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n|-------|-------------------|--------------|\n| **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n| **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n| **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n\nNarrowing is the most common and least visible, because it is easy to mistake for\nrestraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\ndeliver*.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n\nLaw 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\nInside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n\n- The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n- **Direct callers the change would break:** a change that leaves the tree broken is\n  not finished.\n- The test covering the behavior you changed.\n- Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n\nAnything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n\n---\n\n## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n\n- Adding a dependency.\n- Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n- Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n- A rename or move that touches other files.\n- A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n- Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n- Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n\n---\n\n## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n\nYou will find real problems outside scope. Finding them is good; fixing them silently\nis not.\n\n1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n\nFold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\nincorrect, in which case it was never adjacent, it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n\nThe protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\nfile. Read it there. This section deliberately does not restate it: a hard blocker\ncopied into a tier-3 document reads as though an operator instruction could outrank it.\nWhat this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n\nDo not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\nsame response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\nThe operator decides.\n\n---\n\n## 7. FINISHING\n\n`AGENTS.md` §3 Ownership & Completion binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\nincomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. Two\nthings are worth stating in scope terms:\n\n- **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n  explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n- Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n  PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n  go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n\n---\n\n## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n\nScope you have not thought through is scope you will discover mid-edit, which is where\ndrift starts. Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\nsay them:\n\n1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n   pre-write pass, and it is not repeated here, but *which* files is a scope decision,\n   because the set you read is the set you will feel entitled to change.\n2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them up front is what catches\n   the case where the plan needs something outside the frozen scope, while it is still\n   cheap to raise.\n3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan;\n   what belongs here is that a change with no named check is a change with no defined\n   edge, and an undefined edge is where \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n\nThree sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\nmid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\nwhen editing it feels easier than asking.\n\n---\n\n## 9. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n- [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n- [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n- [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n- [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n- [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":164,"totalLines":164,"truncated":false}},"title":"repo-rules/scope-discipline.md","time":{"start":1788619850377,"end":1788619850460}},"id":"prt_0720d0285001mvE1M2D6T8hhge","sessionID":"ses_f8df59422ffeuY8ct2HDJCCEpY","messageID":"msg_0720cd961001O61XZ4w9PEjhWG"}}
